# Printed code is POSIX

Listings in chapters 1–11 use `printf`, `NULL`, `exit`, and `FILE *`.
`print`, `nil`, `exits`, and `Biobuf` belong in `plan9/` or chapter 12.

`common.h` is only:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>
```

Canonical copies: `posix/common.h` (printed) and `plan9/common.h` (author swap).
Keep every `ch*/common.h` and `people/common.h` identical to POSIX.

```c
/* bad: mixed dialect */
if (p == nil)
	print("fail\n");

/* good */
if (p == NULL)
	printf("fail\n");
```

Language: C89-ish so 8c still works after a header swap. Declare variables
at the start of a block. No `for (int i = 0)`. `int` on its own line, brace
on the next. Compile with `gcc -Wall -Wextra`. Warnings are the teacher.
If a `.ms` listing disagrees with the `.c` file, trust the `.c` file, then
fix the book.
